=============================================================================
Heretic and Hexen
=============================================================================

Raven Software
The original Heretic and Hexen game code, everything under src/heretic and
src/hexen. Also the original script parser (sc_man), written for Heretic 2 by
Ben Gokey.

Simon "fraggle" Howard
Chocolate Heretic and Chocolate Hexen, which is the basis of the src/heretic
and src/hexen trees here. Also, from Chocolate Doom: the text mode screen
emulation (src/textscreen), the OPL emulation layer and MIDI file parsing
(MUSIC/opl, MUSIC/opl_queue, MUSIC/midifile, MUSIC/oplplayer), the in-memory
IO shim (memio), MUS to MIDI conversion (mus2mid), and the file globbing API
(i_glob).


=============================================================================
Sound and music
=============================================================================

Nicholai Main (haleyjd's "Chocolate Strife" collaborator, natt)
The music player backends: FluidSynth (flplayer), libmad (madplayer),
PortMidi (portmidiplayer) and Ogg Vorbis (vorbisplayer), the player interface
they share (musicplayer.h), and the video/audio capture support (i_capture).

Ben Ryves <benryves@benryves.com>
The MUS to MIDI converter (mus2mid), written for Chocolate Doom.

ceski
Tracker music playback through libxmp (MUSIC/xmpplayer).

Fabian Greffrath
Sound loading through libsndfile (SDL/i_sndfile).


=============================================================================
Third-party code
=============================================================================

These files are not under the GPL, or are under a different copyright, and
they keep their own notice in the source because their licences require the
notice to travel with the code. Do not strip these headers.

BSD-style licences
------------------
  src/scanner.cpp, src/scanner.h
    Copyright (c) 2010 Braden "Blzut3" Obrzut <admin@maniacsvault.net>
    3-clause BSD. The lexer used by the UMAPINFO and UDMF parsers.

  src/gl_sky.c
    Copyright 2003 Tim Stump, 2005 Christoph Oelckers, 2009 Andrey Budko
    BSD-style with a mandatory source disclosure clause. Sky rendering,
    loosely based on the JDoom and ZDoomGL skies.

  src/gl_clipper.c
    Copyright 2003 Tim Stump
    BSD-style. Visibility checks, loosely based on the JDoom clipper.

  src/gl_vertex.c
    Copyright 2006 Christoph Oelckers
    BSD-style, with a GZDoom-specific clause. Vertex handling.

  src/r_patch.c
    Copyright 2004-2006 Randy Heit
    BSD-style. Patch and composite texture handling.

GNU Lesser General Public License
---------------------------------
  src/umapinfo.cpp, src/umapinfo.h
    Copyright 2017 Christoph Oelckers
    LGPL v2 or later. The UMAPINFO parser.

  src/MUSIC/opl3.c, src/MUSIC/opl3.h, src/MUSIC/wf_rom.h
    Copyright (C) 2013-2020 Nuke.YKT
    Copyright (C) 2026 Tony Gies (Nuked-OPL3-fast modifications)
    LGPL v2.1 or later. The Nuked OPL3 emulator, in Tony Gies' bit-exact
    performance-optimised fork (Nuked-OPL3-fast). The header of opl3.c also
    documents the fork's changes and thanks the MAME development team
    (Jarek Burczynski, Tatsuyuki Satoh), carbon14 and opl3 of
    forums.submarine.org.uk, Matthew Gambrell and Olli Niemitalo of OPLx
    decapsulated, and John McMaster and digshadow of siliconpr0n.org.

Public domain
-------------
  src/md5.c, src/md5.h
    The MD5 message-digest algorithm, due to Ron Rivest, written by
    Colin Plumb in 1993 and placed in the public domain, with later changes
    by Ian Jackson <ian@chiark.greenend.org.uk>.

  src/win_opendir.c, src/win_opendir.h
    POSIX opendir for Visual C++, James Haley, 2006. Public domain, derived
    from the MinGW C Library Extensions Source (also public domain).

Other
-----
  src/xs_Float.h
    Fast float-to-int conversion, from Sree Kotay's article
    "Know Your FPU: Fixing Floating Fast"
    (http://www.stereopsis.com/sree/fpu2006.html).

  src/textscreen/fonts/normal.h
    Generated font data. See src/textscreen/fonts/README for its copyright.
